{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli also known as Raymond of Antioch was a nobleman from the Crusader states in the Levant He was born around 1140 and died in 1187 He was the count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 He was the only son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem\n\nRaymond III succeeded his father as count of Tripoli in 1152 when he was around 12 years old His mother Hodierna served as his regent until he came of age In 1156 Raymond III allied himself with Nur al-Din the Muslim ruler of Aleppo and Damascus against the Byzantine Empire in the Battle of Artah\n\nIn 1164 Raymond III was captured by Nur al-Din during the Battle of Harim and was held prisoner for two years until he was ransomed by the Templars In 1174 Raymond III supported the election of his cousin Baldwin IV of Jerusalem as king of Jerusalem against the claims of Prince Bohemond III of Antioch\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the defense of the Crusader states against the Muslim forces led by Saladin In 1177 Raymond III fought alongside King Baldwin IV and the Templars in the Battle of Montgisard where they defeated Saladin's army In 1179 Raymond III was defeated by Saladin in the Battle of Marj Ayyun but was able to retreat to the safety of the Crusader fortress of Beaufort\n\nIn 1183 Raymond III supported the election of Sibylla of Jerusalem as queen of Jerusalem against the claims of Isabella of Jerusalem He also supported the marriage of Sibylla to Guy of Lusignan who later became king of Jerusalem\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin where the Crusader forces were defeated by Saladin's army Raymond III's death marked the end of the County of Tripoli as an independent state and its eventual incorporation into the Kingdom of Jerusalem\n\n*Notable events during Raymond III's reign*\n\n- Battle of Artah (1156)\n- 